.\" Manual page for fastless.
.\" Lint with: groff -man -ww -z doc/fastless.1
.TH FASTLESS 1 2026-10-23 "fastless 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fastless \- a less-compatible pager for very large and compressed files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fastless
.RI [ options ]
.RB [ + \fIcmd ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B fastless
.RI [ options ]
.B \-\-find
.I pattern
.RB [ \-\-all ]
.RB [ \-\-offset
.IR byte ]
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fastless
is a pager with the commands, options, prompts, and key bindings of
.BR less (1),
designed for searching files of one to hundreds of gigabytes.
Plain files are mapped into memory read-only, never copied, and no line index
is built, so a 200\ GB file opens at once and costs no memory beyond the pages
the operating system caches.
Searches over long distances run on several threads, and literal searches use
AVX2 instructions where the CPU has them.
.PP
gzip, zstd, xz, and bzip2 input is recognised by its first bytes, whatever the
file is called, and decoded before it is shown: into memory, or into a
temporary file once it exceeds
.BR \-\-max\-mem .
Large files decode on several threads, and every integrity check is verified;
damaged or truncated input is an error, never a partial file.
.PP
Without a
.IR file ,
or with
.BR \- ,
standard input is read, and keys are read from
.IR /dev/tty .
Pipes are shown while they are still being written, as in
.BR less .
When standard output is not a terminal,
.B fastless
copies its input (decoded, if it was compressed) to standard output, as
.BR cat (1)
would.
.PP
Positions, byte offsets, and percentages always refer to the decoded data.
Press
.B h
in the pager for a summary of the commands.
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH COMMANDS
Commands are those of
.BR less .
Those marked with * may be preceded by a number,
.IR N ,
typed before the command.
.B ^X
means Control-X, and
.B ESC\-x
means Escape followed by x.
.SS Moving
.TP 22
.BR SPACE " " f " " ^F " " ^V " " PageDown " *"
Forward one window, or
.I N
lines.
.TP
.BR b " " ^B " " ESC\-v " " PageUp " *"
Backward one window, or
.I N
lines.
.TP
.BR z " / " w " *"
Forward / backward one window, setting the window size to
.IR N .
.TP
.BR ESC\-SPACE " *"
Forward one window, even at the end of the file.
.TP
.BR j " " e " " RETURN " " ^E " " ^N " " Down " *"
Forward one line, or
.I N
lines.
.TP
.BR k " " y " " ^Y " " ^P " " ^K " " Up " *"
Backward one line, or
.I N
lines.
.TP
.BR J " / " K " " Y " *"
Forward / backward a line, even past either end.
.TP
.BR d " " ^D " / " u " " ^U " *"
Forward / backward half a window, setting the half-window size to
.IR N .
.TP
.BR ESC\-) " " Right " / " ESC\-( " " Left " *"
Right / left half a screen width, or
.I N
columns.
Long lines are never wrapped, so this is how their ends are seen.
.TP
.BR ESC\-} " " ^Right " / " ESC\-{ " " ^Left
Right to the end of the longest line shown / back to the first column.
.TP
.B F
Forward forever, like
.BR "tail \-f" ;
a regular file is remapped as it grows.
Any key stops following.
.TP
.B ESC\-F
Like
.BR F ,
but stop when a match for the search pattern appears.
.TP
.BR r " " ^R " " ^L
Repaint the screen.
.TP
.B R
Repaint, first taking in any growth of the file.
.SS Jumping
.TP 22
.BR g " " < " " ESC\-< " " Home " *"
First line, or line
.IR N .
.TP
.BR G " " > " " ESC\-> " " End " *"
Last line, or line
.IR N .
.TP
.BR p " " % " *"
.I N
percent into the file; decimals such as
.B 37.5%
are allowed.
.TP
.BR P " *"
Byte offset
.IR N .
(fastless)
.TP
.BR { " " ( " " [ " *"
Find the bracket closing the first such bracket on the top line.
.TP
.BR } " " ) " " ] " *"
Find the bracket opening the last such bracket on the bottom line.
.TP
.BR ESC\-^F " \fIa b\fP / " ESC\-^B " \fIa b\fP"
The same for any pair of characters
.I a
and
.IR b .
.TP
.BI m x \fR\ /\ \fPM x
Mark the top / bottom line with the letter
.IR x .
.TP
.BI \(aq x \fR\ \ \fP^X^X x
Go to mark
.IR x .
.B \(aq\(aq
returns to the previous position, and
.B \(aq^
and
.B \(aq$
are the ends of the file.
.TP
.BI ESC\-m x
Clear mark
.IR x .
.SS Searching
.TP 22
.BI / pattern " *"
Search forward for the
.IR N th
line containing a match.
.TP
.BI ? pattern " *"
Search backward.
.TP
.BR n " / " N " *"
Repeat the search in the same / the opposite direction.
.TP
.BR ESC\-n " / " ESC\-N " *"
Repeat the search, continuing into the following / preceding files.
.TP
.BI & pattern
Show only the lines that match;
.B &
alone shows all lines again.
.TP
.BR ESC\-u " / " ESC\-U
Toggle highlighting of matches / forget the pattern.
.TP
.BR ESC\-r " / " ESC\-l " / " ESC\-w
Switch to regular-expression / literal / glob searching (fastless).
The current pattern is recompiled for the new mode.
.SS Files
.TP 22
.BR :e " [\fIfile\fP] " ^X^V
Examine a new file;
.B :e
alone rereads the current one.
.TP
.BR :n " / " :p " *"
The next / previous file named on the command line.
.TP
.BR :x " *"
The first (or
.IR N th)
file.
.TP
.B :d
Remove the current file from the list.
.TP
.BR = " " ^G " " :f
Show the file name, position, and size.
.SS Miscellaneous
.TP 22
.BI \- x \fR\ \ \fP\-\- name
Toggle or set an option;
.BI \-+ x
resets it and
.BI \-! x
sets its opposite.
.TP
.BI _ x \fR\ \ \fP__ name
Show the setting of an option.
.TP
.BI + cmd
Run
.I cmd
each time a new file is examined.
.TP
.BI ! command
Run a shell command with
.BR $SHELL .
.B %
is replaced by the file name,
.B #
by the previous file, and
.B !!
repeats the last command.
.TP
.BI # command
The same, expanding prompt escapes first.
.TP
.BI | x\ command
Pipe the text between mark
.I x
(or
.BR ^ ,
.BR $ ,
.BR . )
and the screen into
.IR command .
.TP
.BI s \ file
Save the input to
.IR file :
the decoded data for compressed input.
.TP
.B v
Edit the file with
.BR $VISUAL ,
.BR $EDITOR ,
or
.BR vi (1);
.B LESSEDIT
is honoured.
.TP
.BR h " " H " / " V
Show the help / the version.
.TP
.BR q " " Q " " :q " " :Q " " ZZ
Quit, or leave the help.
.TP
.B ^C
Cancel a search, count, wait, or
.BR F .
.TP
.B ^Z
Suspend.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-mouse ,
the wheel scrolls and a click sets mark
.BR # .
.SS Line editing
Prompts for patterns, file names, and commands have
.BR less 's
line editing: Left/Right and
.BR ESC\-h / ESC\-l ,
word moves with ^Left/^Right and
.BR ESC\-b / ESC\-w ,
Home/End and
.BR ESC\-0 / ESC\-$ ,
Delete and
.BR ESC\-x ,
word deletion with ESC\-BACKSPACE and
.BR ESC\-X ,
.B ^U
to clear the line,
.B ^V
to enter the next character literally, and Up/Down for history, matching what
was typed.
BACKSPACE on an empty prompt,
.BR ^G ,
or a lone ESC cancels.
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH SEARCHING
There are three search modes.
.TP
.BR "Regular expressions" " (" \-\-regex ", the default)"
POSIX extended regular expressions, matched within each line without its
newline.
The longest run of plain characters that every match must contain is looked
for first, with the fast literal matcher, and the expression runs only on the
lines that hold it; for alternatives such as
.BR timeout|refused ,
a run from each alternative is looked for.
A single line longer than 2\ GiB cannot be matched by a regular expression.
.TP
.BR Literal " (" \-\-literal )
The pattern's bytes, anywhere, including across line ends.
This is the fastest mode.
.TP
.BR Glob " (" \-\-glob )
A pattern for the whole line, in which
.B *
matches any bytes,
.B ?
one byte, and
.B \e
quotes the next character; use
.BI * text *
to find
.I text
anywhere in a line.
Bracket classes are not supported.
.PP
A match is reported at its byte offset, and the line holding it is moved to
the jump target
.RB ( \-j ,
the top line by default).
All visible matches are highlighted, including overlapping ones.
A new forward search starts at the target line,
.B \-a
starts it after the screen and
.B \-A
after the target line.
.PP
.B \-i
ignores case when the pattern has no capital letters,
.B \-I
always, and
.B \-\-case\-sensitive
never.
Case folding is ASCII only.
Files and patterns are bytes: a UTF\-8 pattern matches its encoding.
.PP
As in
.BR less ,
a pattern may begin with modifiers:
.TP 12
.BR ^N " or " !
Find lines that do not match.
.TP
.BR ^E " or " *
Continue through the following (or preceding) files.
.TP
.BR ^F " or " @
Start at the first file (for
.BR / )
or the last (for
.BR ? ).
.TP
.B ^K
Highlight the matches without moving.
.TP
.B ^R
Search literally, for this pattern only.
.TP
.BI ^S \ n
Match parenthesised subexpression
.I n
and highlight only it.
.TP
.B ^W
Wrap around to the other end of the file if nothing is found.
.TP
.B ^L
Take the next character literally.
.PP
The punctuation forms
.BR ! ,
.BR * ,
and
.B @
are modifiers only in regular-expression mode, because glob and literal
patterns often begin with them.
.B \-\-search\-options
sets modifiers for every search.
.PP
Searches that reach more than 8\ MiB from where they start are divided among
threads in 4\ MiB pieces; the first match in file order is still the one
reported.
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH OPTIONS
Options are read first from the
.B LESS
environment variable, then from the command line.
A long option may be shortened to any unique prefix, and most can also be
changed inside the pager with the
.B \-
command.
.SS Searching
.TP
.BR \-\-regex ", " \-\-literal ", " \-\-glob
Select the search mode (fastless).
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-ignore\-case
Ignore case in searches whose pattern has no capital letters.
.TP
.BR \-I ", " \-\-IGNORE\-CASE
Ignore case in all searches.
.TP
.B \-\-case\-sensitive
Turn
.B \-i
and
.B \-I
off (fastless).
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-search\-skip\-screen
Start forward searches after the last line on the screen.
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-SEARCH\-SKIP\-SCREEN
Start forward searches just after the target line.
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-hilite\-search
Highlight only the match found, not every match.
.TP
.BR \-G ", " \-\-HILITE\-SEARCH
Highlight nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-incsearch
Search as the pattern is typed.
.TP
.BI \-\-search\-options= letters
Modifiers applied to every search: any of
.B EFKNRW
for ^E, ^F, ^K, ^N, ^R, and ^W;
.B \-
clears those before it.
.TP
.BI \-p \ pattern \fR,\fP\ \-\-pattern= pattern
Search for
.I pattern
when the first file opens.
.TP
.BI \-j \ n \fR,\fP\ \-\-jump\-target= n
The screen line that jumps and searches put the target on: a line number
(1 is the top; negative counts from the bottom) or a fraction such as
.B .5
for the middle.
.TP
.BI \-\-match\-shift= n
Columns kept to the left of a match that has to be scrolled into view.
.SS Display
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-RAW\-CONTROL\-CHARS ", " \-r ", " \-\-raw\-control\-chars
Pass ANSI color sequences through.
.B \-r
is the same as
.BR \-R .
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-LINE\-NUMBERS
Show a line number before each line.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-line\-numbers
Show no line numbers at all, in prompts either.
.TP
.BI \-\-line\-num\-width= n
The width of the line-number column (default 7).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-number\-headers
Do not number header lines.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-squeeze\-blank\-lines
Show runs of blank lines as one.
.TP
.BI \-x \ n\fR[\fP,n\fR...],\fP\ \-\-tabs= n\fR[\fP,n\fR...]\fP
Tab stops (default 8).
.TP
.BR \-~ ", " \-\-tilde
Do not mark lines after the end of the file with
.BR ~ .
.TP
.BI \-# \ n \fR,\fP\ \-\-shift= n
Columns to scroll horizontally: a number, a fraction of the screen, or 0 for
half the screen (the default).
.TP
.BI \-\-header= L\fR[\fP,C\fR[\fP,N\fR]]\fP
Keep
.I L
lines, starting at line
.IR N ,
at the top of the screen and
.I C
columns at the left.
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-search\-header\-lines ", " \-\-no\-search\-header\-columns ", " \-\-no\-search\-headers
Leave the header lines, columns, or both out of searches.
.TP
.BR \-J ", " \-\-status\-column
Show a column marking marks and lines with matches.
.TP
.BI \-\-status\-col\-width= n
The width of that column (default 2).
.TP
.B \-\-status\-line
Highlight whole marked lines.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-underline\-special ", " \-U ", " \-\-UNDERLINE\-SPECIAL
Show backspaces and carriage returns (and, for
.BR \-U ,
tabs) as control characters instead of acting on them.
.TP
.BR \-\-proc\-backspace ", " \-\-proc\-return ", " \-\-proc\-tab
Process that character (overstrike, hide a carriage return before a newline,
expand tabs) whatever
.B \-u
says.
.TP
.BR \-\-PROC\-BACKSPACE ", " \-\-PROC\-RETURN ", " \-\-PROC\-TAB
Show that character as a control character whatever
.B \-u
says.
.TP
.BI \-\-rscroll= c
The character marking a chopped line in the last column (default
.BR > ;
.B \-
for none).
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-hilite\-unread ", " \-W ", " \-\-HILITE\-UNREAD
Highlight the first new line after moving forward a page, or after any forward
movement.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-long\-prompt ", " \-M ", " \-\-LONG\-PROMPT
Use
.BR less 's
medium or long prompt instead of fastless's status line.
.TP
.BI \-P prompt \fR,\fP\ \-\-prompt= prompt
Set a prompt:
.BI \-Ps ...
(or no letter) the short one,
.BR \-Pm ,
.BR \-PM ,
.B \-P=
for the
.B =
message,
.B \-Ph
for the help screen, and
.B \-Pw
for waiting at the end of a pipe.
See
.BR PROMPTS .
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-clear\-screen ", " \-C ", " \-\-CLEAR\-SCREEN
Repaint by clearing the screen rather than scrolling.
.TP
.BR \-X ", " \-\-no\-init
Draw on the normal screen, not the terminal's alternate screen.
.TP
.B \-\-redraw\-on\-quit
Leave the final screen on the terminal when quitting.
.TP
.BI \-z \ n \fR,\fP\ \-\-window= n
The window size for scrolling; negative counts back from the screen height.
.TP
.BR \-\-mouse ", " \-\-MOUSE
Scroll with the mouse wheel;
.B \-\-MOUSE
reverses the direction.
.TP
.BI \-\-wheel\-lines= n
Lines per wheel step (default 1).
.SS Quitting and the bell
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-quit\-at\-eof
Quit on a forward command at the end of the file.
.TP
.BR \-E ", " \-\-QUIT\-AT\-EOF
Quit as soon as the end of the file is shown.
.TP
.BR \-F ", " \-\-quit\-if\-one\-screen
Print a file that fits on the screen and exit; a pipe is waited for until it
is known to fit.
.TP
.BR \-K ", " \-\-quit\-on\-intr
Quit with status 2 on ^C.
.TP
.BI \-\-intr= c
The character that cancels like ^C (default ^X).
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet ", " \-\-silent
Ring the bell only for errors, not for moving past either end.
.TP
.BR \-Q ", " \-\-QUIET ", " \-\-SILENT
Never ring the bell.
.SS Input
.TP
.BI \-\-compression= format
.BR auto " (the default), " none ", " gzip ", " zstd ", " xz ", or " bzip2 .
.B none
shows the bytes as stored (fastless).
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-mem= GiB
Keep decoded or piped input in memory up to this many GiB (default 10, and
fractions such as
.B 0.5
are allowed); beyond it, input goes to a temporary file.
.B 0
always uses a file (fastless).
.TP
.BI \-\-tmpdir= dir
Where temporary files go (fastless).
.TP
.BI \-\-threads= n
Threads for searching, counting lines, and decoding: 1 to 256, or
0 (the default) for one per CPU, at most 32 (fastless).
.TP
.BR \-\-simd= auto | off | avx2
Whether literal searches and the threaded gzip decoder use fastless's AVX2 code;
.B avx2
is an error on a CPU without it (fastless).
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Open devices and other non-regular files.
Named pipes need no
.BR \-f .
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-no\-lessopen
Ignore
.BR LESSOPEN .
.TP
.B \-\-show\-preproc\-errors
Report an input preprocessor that fails.
.TP
.B \-\-follow\-name
Make
.B F
reopen the file by name if it is replaced, as for rotated logs.
.TP
.BI \-\-lesskey\-src= file
Read key bindings from a
.BR lesskey (1)
source file.
.TP
.BI \-\-lesskey\-content= text
Key bindings given as lesskey source, with lines separated by newlines or
semicolons (\fB\e;\fP is a semicolon).
.TP
.B \-\-save\-marks
Keep marks in the history file between sessions.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-histdups
Drop earlier copies of a command from the history.
.TP
.BI + cmd
Run
.I cmd
when the first file opens, as in
.BR +G ,
.BR +/error ,
or
.BR +50% ;
.BI ++ cmd
runs it for every file.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-? ", " \-\-help
Print a summary of the options and exit.
.SS Noninteractive searching
.TP
.BI \-\-find= pattern
Print the byte offset of the first match and exit, without starting the pager
(fastless).
.TP
.B \-\-all
With
.BR \-\-find ,
print every match, including overlapping ones.
.TP
.BI \-\-offset= byte
Start the search (or the pager) at this byte offset (fastless).
.SS Accepted without effect
.BR \-b " (" \-\-buffers ),
.BR \-B " (" \-\-auto\-buffers ),
.BR \-d " (" \-\-dumb ),
.BR \-h " (" \-\-max\-back\-scroll ),
.BR \-S " (" \-\-chop\-long\-lines ),
.BR \-y " (" \-\-max\-forw\-scroll ),
.BR \-\-file\-size ,
.BR \-\-no\-keypad ,
.BR \-\-no\-vbell ,
and
.B \-\-exit\-follow\-on\-close
are accepted for compatibility and ignored.
Lines are always chopped, as with
.BR \-S .
.SS Not supported
.BR \-o ", " \-\-log\-file ", " \-O ", " \-\-LOG\-FILE " (log files),"
.BR \-t ", " \-\-tag ", " \-T ", " \-\-tag\-file " (tags),"
.BR \-D ", " \-\-color ", " \-\-use\-color " (colors),"
.BR \-\-wordwrap ,
.BR \-\-modelines ,
.BR \-\(dq ", " \-\-quotes ,
.BR \-\-use\-backslash ,
and
.BR \-k ", " \-\-lesskey\-file " (compiled lesskey files)"
are rejected with a message.
In
.BR LESS ,
unknown options are ignored.
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH PROMPTS
The default status line is fastless's own: the file name, the position, the
search mode, and a reminder of the main keys.
.BR \-m ,
.BR \-M ,
and
.B \-P
select
.BR less 's
prompts and its prompt language: %-escapes such as
.BR %f " (file name), " %b " (byte offset), " %B " (size), " %p " (percent), " %l " and " %L " (line numbers), " %i " and " %m " (file index and count),"
.BR %c ", " %d ", " %D ", " %E ", " %F ", " %g ", " %P ", " %s ", " %t ", " %T ", and " %x ,
and
.BI ? x ...:...\&.
conditionals.
A line-number item shows
.B ?
and its condition is false until the number is known.
.PP
Line numbers are counted only when something on the screen shows one, with
SIMD on every search thread, and remembered per 64\ MiB piece.
A long jump into uncounted data does not wait: the numbers appear once they
are counted.
^C during counting turns line numbers off, as in
.BR less .
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH COMPRESSED INPUT
gzip members, zstd frames (including skippable frames), xz streams, and bzip2
streams may be concatenated; only zero bytes may follow the last one.
The whole input is decoded, and its checksums verified, before the first page
is shown; standard error shows progress, and ^C cancels.
.PP
Decoded data is kept in memory until it exceeds
.BR \-\-max\-mem ,
then moves to an unlinked temporary file with mode 0600 in
.BR \-\-tmpdir ,
.BR $TMPDIR ,
or
.IR /tmp .
There must be room for the whole decoded size, which can be far larger than
the compressed file.
.I /tmp
is often held in memory on Linux; choose a directory on disk for large files.
Switching to another file releases the decoded data, and returning decodes it
again.
.PP
xz files decode on several threads when they hold several blocks, as threaded
encoders write them.
bzip2 decodes on every thread with the block decoder of
.BR lbzip2 (1).
gzip files of 2\ MiB or more decode on every thread when there are at least 4:
each thread starts at a deflate block it finds in its own part of the file,
decoding it twice until the 32\ KiB before it are known; output waiting
for its place in memory is held to 32\ MiB per thread.
zstd files of several frames, as
.BR pzstd (1)
writes them, decode on every thread; a file of one frame, as
.B "zstd \-T0"
writes, decodes on one.
Archive members, such as those of a tar file, are not extracted.
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH PIPES AND STANDARD INPUT
Standard input, named pipes, and devices are read by a background thread and
paged while they are still being written, so
.B "make 2>&1 | fastless"
and
.B "tail \-f log | fastless"
show output as it arrives.
While a stream is being read its size is unknown: the status says
.BR (reading) ,
and the prompt conditions
.BR ?s ", " ?B ", " ?p ", " ?P ", " ?L ", " ?D ", and " ?e
are false.
.PP
Moving past the end waits for more input;
.BR G ,
.BR % ,
and
.B \(aq$
follow the end until the input ends; forward searches go on into input as it
arrives; and jumps past what has arrived wait for it.
Any key ends the wait.
Up to
.B \-\-max\-mem
(at least 64\ MiB) is read ahead; beyond that, input is read only as far as
the screen, a search, or a jump needs, so
.B "yes | fastless"
does not fill the disk.
A compressed stream is read whole, then decoded.
.PP
A pipe cannot be read twice, so it stays open, with what was read, while
another file is shown.
When standard output is not a terminal, a pipe is copied through as it
arrives and not kept.
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH INPUT PREPROCESSORS
.B LESSOPEN
and
.B LESSCLOSE
work as in
.BR less :
.BI | cmd\ %s
pages the output of
.I cmd
(the file itself, if that is empty),
.BI || cmd\ %s
treats empty output with status 0 as an empty file, and
.I cmd\ %s
prints the name of a replacement file, which
.B LESSCLOSE
is later given with the original name.
Files that begin with gzip, zstd, xz, or bzip2 magic skip the preprocessor,
because the built-in decoders are faster;
.B "\-L \-\-compression=none"
shows their raw bytes.
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH KEY BINDINGS
Keys can be rebound with
.BR lesskey (1)
source:
.BR \-\-lesskey\-src ,
or else the first of
.BR $LESSKEYIN ,
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/lesskey ,
.IR ~/.config/lesskey ,
and
.IR ~/.lesskey ;
then
.B LESSKEY_CONTENT
and
.BR \-\-lesskey\-content .
The
.BR #command ,
.BR #line\-edit ,
and
.B #env
sections,
.BR #stop ,
.B \ek
key names, and extra strings work as in
.BR lesskey .
Besides
.BR less 's
actions, fastless has
.BR regex\-mode ,
.BR literal\-mode ,
and
.BR glob\-mode ;
the tag and OSC\ 8 hyperlink actions are accepted but do nothing.
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH NONINTERACTIVE SEARCH
.B \-\-find
prints the zero-based byte offset of the first match in the decoded file, or
with
.B \-\-all
of every match, one per line, and exits.
Glob mode prints one offset per matching line.
.B LESSOPEN
is not used.
A pipe is read to its end first.
.PP
.EX
fastless \-\-find \(aq^ERROR.*timeout\(aq huge.log
fastless \-\-literal \-\-find needle \-\-all huge.log.zst
fastless \-\-literal \-\-find needle \-\-offset 150000000000 huge.log
.EE
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP 18
.B LESS
Options read before the command line.
String values end at
.BR $ .
.TP
.BR LESSOPEN ", " LESSCLOSE
Input preprocessors.
.TP
.B LESSEDIT
The editor command for
.BR v ,
in prompt language (default
.BR "%E ?lm+%lm. %g" ).
.TP
.BR VISUAL ", " EDITOR
The editor (default
.BR vi );
an empty value counts as unset.
.TP
.B SHELL
The shell for
.BR ! ,
.BR # ,
and
.BR | .
.TP
.B LESSSECURE
When 1, disables shell commands, pipes, saving, editing,
.BR :e ,
preprocessors, and history writing.
.TP
.B LESSCHARSET
If set,
.B utf\-8
shows text as UTF\-8 and any other value shows bytes above 127 as
.BI < XX >\fR.\fP
If not, the locale
.RB ( LC_ALL ,
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
.BR LANG )
decides.
.TP
.BR LESSKEYIN ", " LESSKEY_CONTENT ", " XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Key bindings; see
.BR "KEY BINDINGS" .
.TP
.B FASTLESSHISTFILE
The history file (default
.IR ~/.fastlesshst ;
.B \-
for none).
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Temporary files, unless
.B \-\-tmpdir
is given.
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH FILES
.TP 18
.I ~/.fastlesshst
Search and shell history, and marks with
.BR \-\-save\-marks .
.TP
.IR ~/.lesskey ", " ~/.config/lesskey
Key bindings in lesskey source form.
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP 6
.B 0
Success; for
.BR \-\-find ,
a match was found.
.TP
.B 1
.B \-\-find
found no match.
.TP
.B 2
An error, such as a file that cannot be opened or damaged compressed data, or
^C with
.BR \-K .
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted by ^C outside the pager, such as while decoding or during
.BR \-\-find .
.TP
.BI 128+ n
Ended by signal
.IR n ,
such as SIGTERM.
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH DIFFERENCES FROM LESS
fastless follows
.B less
668 wherever it can.
It differs in these ways.
.SS Additions
.IP \(bu 3
Literal and whole-line glob search modes, alongside regular expressions
.RB ( \-\-literal ,
.BR \-\-glob ,
.BR ESC\-r ", " ESC\-l ", " ESC\-w ).
.IP \(bu
Built-in, multithreaded gzip, zstd, xz, and bzip2 decoding, chosen by content
rather than by file name, with
.BR \-\-compression ,
.BR \-\-max\-mem ,
and
.BR \-\-tmpdir .
.IP \(bu
Threaded searches and line counting
.RB ( \-\-threads )
and AVX2 literal matching
.RB ( \-\-simd ).
.IP \(bu
.BR \-\-find ,
.BR \-\-all ,
and
.B \-\-offset
for searching without the pager, and
.B P
to jump to a byte offset.
.IP \(bu
Its own default status line, with the file, position, and search mode.
.SS Behaviour
.IP \(bu 3
Long lines are always chopped and never wrapped:
.B \-S
is the only behaviour, and
.B \-\-wordwrap
is not supported.
.IP \(bu
Line numbers are counted lazily, only when shown, and a long jump does not
wait for them;
.B less
counts lines as it reads.
.IP \(bu
Literal searches match across line ends.
Regular expressions are POSIX extended expressions from the C library, matched
in the C locale, so case folding is ASCII only.
.IP \(bu
Search positions and the status line count bytes in the decoded data.
.IP \(bu
Compressed files bypass
.BR LESSOPEN .
Preprocessors are never applied to standard input, even with the
.BI |\- cmd
form of
.BR LESSOPEN .
.IP \(bu
.B \-r
is the same as
.BR \-R :
other control sequences are never sent to the terminal, and
.B \-u
never sends raw backspaces or carriage returns.
Control characters show as bold
.BI ^ X
and other unprintable bytes as
.BI < XX >
or
.BI <U+ XXXX >\fR;\fP
.B LESSBINFMT
and
.B LESSUTFBINFMT
are not used.
.IP \(bu
.B \-?
prints an option summary and exits, rather than showing the help screen.
.IP \(bu
History is kept in
.I ~/.fastlesshst
.RB ( FASTLESSHISTFILE ),
separate from
.BR less 's.
.SS Not supported
.IP \(bu 3
Tags
.RB ( \-t ", " \-T ", " :t ),
log files
.RB ( \-o ", " \-O ),
colors
.RB ( \-D ", " \-\-use\-color ),
.BR \-\-modelines ,
.BR \-\(dq ,
.BR \-\-use\-backslash ,
OSC\ 8 hyperlinks, and compiled lesskey files
.RB ( \-k ).
.IP \(bu
The
.BI \- n
form of
.BI \-z n\fR,\fP
as in
.BR \-20 ,
and capitalising only the first letter of an upper-case long option, as in
.BR \-\-Quit\-at\-eof .
.IP \(bu
Environment variables other than those under
.BR ENVIRONMENT ,
among them
.BR LESSHISTFILE ,
.BR LESS_TERMCAP_* ,
.BR LESSANSIENDCHARS ,
.BR LESSMETACHARS ,
and
.BR LESS_IS_MORE .
.IP \(bu
Character sets other than UTF\-8 and single bytes.
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH NOTES
A mapped file must not be truncated while it is shown, or the program may be
killed by SIGBUS, as with any reader that maps files.
Replacing the file by name does not change what is shown unless
.B F
is used with
.BR \-\-follow\-name .
A cold search still reads every scanned byte from storage; the mapping removes
copies, not I/O.
.PP
fastless needs a 64-bit system with POSIX
.BR REG_STARTEND ,
such as Linux or macOS.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
fastless huge.log
fastless \-\-literal \-I \(aq+/connection reset\(aq huge.log.zst
LESS=\-iR fastless build.log
journalctl \-b | fastless +G
fastless \-\-max\-mem 0 \-\-tmpdir /scratch dump.xz
fastless \-\-find \(aqpanic:\(aq \-\-all kernel.log.bz2
.EE
